<% content_for :stylesheets, area_stylesheet_link_tags("simulator") %>

<header class="page-hero">
  <h1>Nearby traffic, in your pocket.</h1>
  <p class="page-hero-subtitle">skyBlip Go transmits your position in ADS-L and shows ADS-L and FLARM traffic. skyBlip Bravo detects Mode S and ADS-B transponders. Together they make skyBlip Tango. Aircraft with ADS-B transmit their position by radio.</p>
</header>

<section id="journey" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">From the first try to your first flight</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Whether you fly a light aircraft, a microlight, a glider or a paraglider, we&rsquo;ll guide you at each step.</p>
  <ol class="steps-grid steps-grid--journey">
    <li>
      <h3><%= link_to_page "skyblip-go", label: "Try it" %></h3>
      <p>Read the traffic at a glance. Try the radar and its alert in the simulator.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3><%= link_to_page "buy", label: "Get one" %></h3>
      <p>Choose an off-the-shelf device. The skyBlip software is free, with no subscription.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3><%= link_to_page "install", label: "Install it" %></h3>
      <p>Plug the device into your computer. Briefing guides you and shows you where to press, on a photo or on the device&rsquo;s screen.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <%= render "shared/device", device: :go, label: t("briefing.figures.go_reset"), marks: { reset: t("briefing.figures.reset_twice") } %>
  <p class="signature"><strong>You&rsquo;re ready to fly.</strong> The <%= link_to_page "guides", label: "first steps guide for your device" %> shows you what to check before you set off.</p>
</section>

<section id="where-to-look" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Where to look</h2>
  <p>The radar shows you where to look for another aircraft. You&rsquo;re at the centre and the direction you&rsquo;re travelling is at the top. A symbol at the top right means traffic ahead and to your right.</p>
  <p>An upward point means the aircraft is above you. A downward point means it&rsquo;s below you. A diamond means it&rsquo;s roughly at your level. The line from the symbol shows its projected track over the next minute.</p>
  <div class="columns">
    <div>
      <%= screen_image_tag "blips/above-steady.png", alt: "" %>
      <p>Above you</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <%= screen_image_tag "blips/level-steady.png", alt: "" %>
      <p>At your level</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <%= screen_image_tag "blips/below-steady.png", alt: "" %>
      <p>Below you</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>When an alert triggers, part of the screen flashes in the direction of the traffic. It shows you where to look. It doesn&rsquo;t tell you to climb or descend.</p>
</section>

<section id="simulator" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">Try the radar</h2>
  <%= render "shared/simulator_loader", loading: :lazy %>
  <p>Find an aircraft. Its shape tells you whether it&rsquo;s above, at your level or below. Follow its track line.</p>
  <p>Tick &quot;With Bravo (skyBlip Tango)&quot; to restart the simulator and see aircraft fitted with ADS-B farther away.</p>
  <p class="signature"><%= link_to_page "skyblip-go", label: "More room to try it", class: "button button--primary" %></p>
</section>

<section id="devices" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">Which device is right for you?</h2>
  <figure class="pair">
    <%= content_image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.avif", alt: "skyBlip Go, a white device with an antenna and traffic radar on its e-paper screen." %>
    <span class="pair-plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
    <%= content_image_tag "pages/skyblip-bravo/seeed-x1.avif", alt: "skyBlip Bravo, a black Seeed SenseCAP MeshTracker X1 about the size of a card.", class: "photo-x1" %>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li><%= link_to_page "features", label: "Go" %> transmits your position in ADS-L, the European standard for sharing position by radio, and shows ADS-L and FLARM traffic. FLARM is a position-sharing system widely used in gliding.</li>
    <li><%= link_to_page "skyblip-bravo", label: "Bravo" %> detects Mode S and ADS-B transponders and sends ADS-B traffic to your navigation app. It doesn&rsquo;t transmit your position.</li>
    <li><%= link_to_page "skyblip-tango", label: "Tango" %> pairs a Go with a Bravo. See all three types of traffic on Go&rsquo;s radar while Go keeps transmitting your position in ADS-L.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="value" class="band band--blip">
  <h2 class="section-title">Off-the-shelf hardware, from €68</h2>
  <p>Buy a LilyGO T-Echo for Go or a MeshTracker X1 for Bravo. You pay for the hardware. The software and updates are free, with no subscription. The code is open source. Anyone can read and check it.</p>
  <div class="quote">
    <blockquote>
      &quot;Safety isn&rsquo;t a premium feature. Our safety features for recreational flying and air sports stay open source and free.&quot;
    </blockquote>
  </div>
  <p class="signature"><%= link_to_page "buy", label: "See the hardware and prices", class: "button button--primary" %> · <%= link_to_page "about", label: "Read our pledge" %></p>
</section>

<section id="questions" class="band">
  <h2 class="section-title">What pilots ask before buying</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Who can see me?</summary>
      <p>Pilots with equipment that can receive your ADS-L signal can see the position your Go transmits. Go receives FLARM but doesn&rsquo;t transmit it. Bravo doesn&rsquo;t transmit your position.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I see aircraft with transponders?</summary>
      <p>Yes, with Bravo. It detects Mode S and ADS-B transponders. Aircraft transmitting ADS-B appear with their position in your navigation app or on a paired Go. Mode S alone gives no position. Bravo detects it, and we&rsquo;re collecting data to work out how best to report it on Go and in navigation apps.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a subscription?</summary>
      <p>No. You buy the hardware. Position transmissions, traffic, alerts and updates need no subscription.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is it certified?</summary>
      <p>No. skyBlip is an uncertified aid to visual lookout. Keep looking outside. Aircraft won&rsquo;t appear unless your device receives a compatible signal. Keep the equipment required for your flight.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a phone?</summary>
      <p>Go has its own screen. With Bravo on its own, use a compatible app on a phone or tablet to see traffic. In a Tango, Go shows it.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I go back to the original software?</summary>
      <p>Yes. On the T-Echo, skyBlip leaves the program that installs the software in place. On the X1, <%= link_to_page "install", label: "save a copy of the original software", anchor: "si-quelque-chose-bloque" %> before replacing it.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
